% ============================================================================
% comet-tail-type — the two separate tails a comet actually has, and the
% defining path each one traces (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling library to the already-shipped `comet-part.adj` (which names
% nucleus/coma/tail as the three PHYSICAL PARTS of a comet) — this table
% zooms into that third part, `tail`, and answers a DIFFERENT, more specific
% question: a comet's tail is not one uniform streamer, it is actually TWO
% separate tails, and each one traces its own characteristic path across the
% sky. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? comet_tail_type(dust_tail, $D)
%       % traces_a_broad_gently_curving_path_away_from_the_sun
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `comet_tail_type(tail, description)` carrying the source citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a comet-related term that is not one of these two tails. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the tail it
% names:
%
%     ? comet_tail_type($T, always_points_directly_away_from_the_sun)
%       % ion_tail
%
% The two tails, as a little truth table:
%
%     tail         description                                              quote (verbatim)
%     -----------  -------------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     dust_tail     traces_a_broad_gently_curving_path_away_from_the_sun     "This dust tail traces a broad, gently curving path away from the Sun."
%     ion_tail      always_points_directly_away_from_the_sun                 "The ion tail always points directly away from the Sun."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from NASA Space Place's "What Is a Comet?"
% page — the SAME page the sibling `comet-part.adj` already cites for
% nucleus/coma/tail. That page states plainly, right after introducing the
% single `tail`, "When astronomers look closely, they find that comets
% actually have two separate tails" and then gives each of the two its own
% complete, standalone sentence. The original authoring note said the
% quotes were "WebFetch-verified before writing"; the raw-page measurement
% below supersedes it. `trust authoritative` — NASA is a primary .gov
% source, the same tier `comet-part.adj` (the sibling library this one
% refines) already uses.
%
% This is genuinely a CLOSED two-way split, not an arbitrary subset of a
% longer list — the source's own sentence states a comet has exactly two
% separate tails. A comet-related term that is NOT one of these two —
% `nucleus`, `coma` — is a different physical part entirely (already tabled
% in `comet-part.adj`, a coarser sibling table, not a tail sub-type), so a
% recall on it here ABSTAINS rather than inventing a tail description.
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SENTENCE (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The
% envelope used to be the dust-tail span, so an ion-tail answer was
% warranted primarily by a sentence about the DUST tail -- while the
% ion-tail sentence sat in a table-level `cites`, riding along as a
% corroboration rather than as that row's warrant. Each row now carries
% the sentence defining its own tail, the `cites` is gone, and the
% envelope is the page's sentence framing the two-tail split:
%
%     "When astronomers look closely, they find that comets actually
%      have two separate tails."
%
% That envelope names neither tail -- not as the phrases "dust tail" or
% "ion tail", and not as the bare tokens "dust" or "ion" -- and shares
% no content word with either row's value. The two row spans are
% mutually exclusive in the same way, so a negative assertion may name a
% whole span without the shared-word hazard `comet-part.adj` has.
%
% Measured 2026-09-16 UTC by this table's own fetch. The page answered
% 200 in 21830 bytes and a nonsense path on the same host answered 404
% with a full styled page of 11281 bytes -- so the control is that the
% spans are absent from a page of COMPARABLE SIZE, not that the page is
% empty. Those byte counts are measured-at-a-moment, not properties of
% the page: the sibling `comet-part.adj` measured the same 404 at 11280
% bytes about an hour earlier. Tags removed without a space and only
% ASCII whitespace collapsed: both row spans and the envelope each occur
% exactly once, inside one `<p>`, scripts set aside and over the whole
% file, with no `<head>` copy. Both row spans sit in the SAME `<p>`.
% Every row's page is the envelope's, so no row restates a `locator`
% line and no row repeats the `trust` tier.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table comet_tail_type {
    columns tail, description

    row (dust_tail, traces_a_broad_gently_curving_path_away_from_the_sun) {
        source "This dust tail traces a broad, gently curving path away from the Sun."
    }
    row (ion_tail, always_points_directly_away_from_the_sun) {
        source "The ion tail always points directly away from the Sun."
    }

    source "When astronomers look closely, they find that comets actually have two separate tails."
    locator "https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/comets/en/"
    trust authoritative
}
